# Input
```ts
/* should generate diagnostics */
const a1 = new Array<string>("x");
const a2 = new Array(1 as number);
const a3 = new Array("x" as string);
const a4 = new Array("x"!);
const a5 = new Array("x" satisfies string);

```

# Diagnostics
```
invalid.ts:2:12 lint/nursery/noNewArray ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  i This new Array() call has a single argument, so its result depends on whether the argument is a number.
  
    1 │ /* should generate diagnostics */
  > 2 │ const a1 = new Array<string>("x");
      │            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    3 │ const a2 = new Array(1 as number);
    4 │ const a3 = new Array("x" as string);
  
  i With a number, new Array() creates an empty array of that length. With any other value, it creates an array that holds the value.
  
  i Use an array literal such as [value] to create an array that holds the value, or Array.from({ length }) to create an array of a given length.
  
  i This rule belongs to the nursery group, which means it is not yet stable and may change in the future. Visit https://biomejs.dev/linter/#nursery for more information.
  

